NEW DEFECT INTRODUCED BY THIS ATTEMPT'S "never a 500" FIX (personsSaveErrorMessage,
handlers_rates.go:111-127, new in attempt 2 -- absent from WS5.1.diff):
`strings.Contains(msg, "birth_month")` is used to detect the MISSING-birth-
month case, but prepare.ValidatePersons (prepare/validate.go:47) emits TWO
distinct errors containing that substring:
  - "persons: invalid birth_month for %q: %w"        (missing/malformed -- correct target)
  - "persons: birth_month %q is after start_date %q"  (PRESENT, just later than start_date)
personsSaveErrorMessage collapses both to "Each person needs a birth month",
which is FALSE for the second case. No client-side `max` ties the birth-
month `<input type="month">` to start_date (grepped
whatif-rate-assumptions.js and rate-assumptions.html: no `.max` set on
birth-month), so this is reachable by an ordinary user (start-date edit
made earlier than an existing person's birth month, or a birth-year typo),
not just a contrived direct POST.

Reproduced against the real production handler (net/http, real form
encoding, real renderError/template output -- not a mock):
  POST /whatif/settings person_birth_month[]=2030-01 start_date=2026-04
  -> status 400, body:
  `<p class="mt-2 text-body-sm text-negative">Each person needs a birth month</p>`
  (full test + run log below). Before this attempt (base and attempt 1),
  this same input produced a 500 whose text was at least accurate
  ("Failed to save settings: persons: birth_month \"2030-01\" is after
  start_date \"2026-04\"") -- this attempt fixed the status code but broke
  the message's truthfulness for this input.

  $ go test ./internal/handlers/whatif/... -run TestZZCheckerProbe_BirthMonthAfterStartDate_EndToEnd -v
  status=400 body=...<p ...>Each person needs a birth month</p>...
  --- FAIL: TestZZCheckerProbe_BirthMonthAfterStartDate_EndToEnd (0.00s)
  (probe test written to my own snapshot only, not committed; removed after use)

TestPersonsSaveErrorMessage (the new unit test for this exact function) has
no case for the "birth_month ... is after start_date ..." error shape --
the gap that let this through. All existing/added WS5 tests still PASS
(`go test ./internal/handlers/whatif/...` -> ok, 15.7s; `node --test
web/static/js/whatif-rate-assumptions.test.cjs` -> 18/18 pass); this is a
narrow, real gap in the new classifier, not a broad regression.

IN-SCOPE: personsSaveErrorMessage is new code this attempt wrote
specifically to satisfy the "never a 500 / clear message" requirement
pulled into scope by ruling 2026-09-24d, and the dispatch's own named
attack surface ("string-prefix error classification ... can a ... error be
misclassified"). A misleading message is not a "clear, user-facing"
message; the fix trades a correct-but-500 error for an incorrect-but-400
one for this input.

No other defect found under the enumerated attack surfaces: the hasPersons
success path (renderRecalc(settings, revision)) and the generic
fallback-error path are structurally identical to the pre-existing
recalcAndRender tail (same failMsg prefix, same statusForMutationError
call) -- confirmed by reading handlers.go's recalcAndRender/renderRecalc
verbatim; no other surface reads glide-path fields (grepped
web/static/js/*.js and web/templates for start_stock_pct/end_stock_pct/
transition_years/GlidePath -- only rate-assumptions.{js,html}); keyboard
activation of the checkbox and Remove buttons uses the same onchange/click
handlers as mouse (no separate path); the TOCTOU race the handler's own
comment documents (a concurrent healthcare link landing between the
pre-check Load and the save reaches ValidatePersons's
"healthcare_persons: person_id ... not found", which personsSaveErrorMessage
does not match by prefix, so it falls to the pre-existing generic 500 --
same as base/attempt 1 in that race, not a new regression).

VERDICT: FAIL -- new, real, user-reachable defect in code this attempt
added to satisfy D7's "never a 500" / clear-message requirement.
